atus). [Data: Entities (2306, 2307, 2308, 2309, 2323); Relationships (4036, 4037, 4038, 4039)]\\n\\nThe text explicitly states PARP inhibitors “work best” in homologous recombination deficiency (HRD)-positive cancers, positioning HRD positivity as a key decision factor for selecting or prioritizing PARPi maintenance strategies. This makes tumor biology (HRD status) a central selector that links the therapy class to patient subgroups rather than broad, non-stratified use. [Data: Entities (2323, 2306); Relationships (4039)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Mechanistic rationale: blocking PARP disrupts cancer cell repair and can lead to cancer cell death\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes a mechanistic chain connecting PARP function to cancer survival. PARP is described as a protein that repairs cancer cells and allows them to survive (i.e., supports cancer cell repair). [Data: Entities (4049, 4050); Relationships (8434)]\\n\\nIn this framework, PARP inhibitors—by blocking PARP—are linked to cancer cell death as an intended therapeutic outcome. This creates a clear, mechanistically grounded narrative: inhibit PARP → impair repair processes → promote cancer-cell death. [Data: Entities (2306, 4051); Relationships (8416)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Olaparib is a named PARP inhibitor with defined maintenance duration and a combination pathway with bevacizumab\",\"explanation\":\"Olaparib (Lynparza) is explicitly identified as a PARP inhibitor and described in the context of maintenance therapy. The text provides duration details: olaparib may be given as monotherapy for up to 2 years, or used with bevacizumab for up to 15 months while olaparib may continue to a total of up to 2 years. [Data: Entities (2307); Relationships (4036)]\\n\\nA specific combination relationship is described between olaparib and bevacizumab for HR-deficient and BRCA-mutated cancers, where maintenance therapy with bevacizumab plus olaparib is recommended when chemotherapy included bevacizumab. This links regimen hi